1
00:00:00,764 --> 00:00:05,569
I've been writing about global health and medical innovation for years,

2
00:00:05,569 --> 00:00:10,407
and when I tell my friends and family how much progress there's been,

3
00:00:10,407 --> 00:00:13,043
the reaction is almost always the same.

4
00:00:13,043 --> 00:00:16,446
They had no idea most of it was happening.

5
00:00:16,446 --> 00:00:20,150
Take the most common cause of death today, heart disease.

6
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:24,288
People today have roughly a quarter the chance of dying from heart disease

7
00:00:24,288 --> 00:00:27,658
as people did in the 1950s at the same age.

8
00:00:27,658 --> 00:00:29,393
A quarter.

9
00:00:29,393 --> 00:00:33,730
Back then, there were no statins, no cholesterol testing,

10
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:36,133
no implanted pacemakers,

11
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:39,670
no anti-smoking campaigns, no bans on trans fats,

12
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:43,507
no bypass surgery, no CPR.

13
00:00:43,507 --> 00:00:44,975
But hardly anyone hears

14
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:48,712
about the long-term impact of those breakthroughs.

15
00:00:48,712 --> 00:00:50,914
When progress happens gradually,

16
00:00:50,914 --> 00:00:53,550
it's rarely considered news.

17
00:00:53,550 --> 00:00:55,185
And I think it's a problem.

18
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:59,423
I think it’s part of why people don’t know how important it’s all been --

19
00:00:59,423 --> 00:01:01,658
public health, vaccines and all the rest --

20
00:01:01,658 --> 00:01:04,528
in improving people’s lives.

21
00:01:04,528 --> 00:01:06,229
But what I want to tell you about

22
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:09,099
is the thing that doesn't make the news at all.

23
00:01:09,099 --> 00:01:10,901
Not the breakthroughs themselves,

24
00:01:10,901 --> 00:01:14,972
but everything that got in the way of them.

25
00:01:14,972 --> 00:01:18,008
I used to have this impression that breakthroughs were uncommon,

26
00:01:18,008 --> 00:01:19,409
sporadic,

27
00:01:19,409 --> 00:01:21,778
sometimes happening by pure chance,

28
00:01:21,778 --> 00:01:25,983
sometimes by sheer determination.

29
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:27,918
The way I see it now,

30
00:01:27,918 --> 00:01:34,091
it's more like there's a continuous stream of medical innovation every year.

31
00:01:34,091 --> 00:01:36,994
In just the last two years, for example,

32
00:01:36,994 --> 00:01:41,331
we've had a new antiviral against HIV,

33
00:01:41,331 --> 00:01:46,770
which protects against infections with an efficacy of nearly 100 percent

34
00:01:46,770 --> 00:01:50,607
with just a single dose given every six months.

35
00:01:50,607 --> 00:01:54,211
There are new drugs that reduce cholesterol levels by 60 percent,

36
00:01:54,211 --> 00:01:57,147
beyond the effect of statins.

37
00:01:57,147 --> 00:02:01,351
There are new treatments that slow down the progression of certain cancers,

38
00:02:01,351 --> 00:02:07,758
certain lung cancers, brain cancer and multiple myeloma by half or more.

39
00:02:07,758 --> 00:02:10,093
And in just the last five years,

40
00:02:10,093 --> 00:02:14,598
we've had new vaccines against four diseases for the very first time.

41
00:02:14,598 --> 00:02:15,766
COVID, of course,

42
00:02:15,766 --> 00:02:18,301
but also the first malaria vaccine,

43
00:02:18,301 --> 00:02:24,508
the first chikungunya vaccine and the first vaccine against RSV.

44
00:02:24,508 --> 00:02:29,479
The tools to develop new drugs and vaccines have improved enormously.

45
00:02:29,479 --> 00:02:31,381
Like genome sequencing, for example,

46
00:02:31,381 --> 00:02:35,085
which has touched almost every part of biology.

47
00:02:35,085 --> 00:02:38,855
Back when the Human Genome Project was completed in 2003,

48
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:43,593
it cost $50 million to sequence one person’s genome,

49
00:02:43,593 --> 00:02:45,395
and it took half a year.

50
00:02:45,395 --> 00:02:48,799
Now it takes under four hours

51
00:02:48,799 --> 00:02:51,468
and it costs a few hundred dollars.

52
00:02:51,468 --> 00:02:55,439
There's also been a revolution in the technology of microscopes.

53
00:02:55,439 --> 00:02:57,874
Over the past 200 years,

54
00:02:57,874 --> 00:03:02,779
their resolution has increased over 10,000-fold.

55
00:03:02,779 --> 00:03:06,950
We can now see viruses down to their individual atoms

56
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:11,455
and design new drugs to target them extremely precisely.

57
00:03:11,455 --> 00:03:14,558
This is RSV, respiratory syncytial virus.

58
00:03:14,558 --> 00:03:16,093
It's in the middle right there.

59
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:19,696
And we can now see its atomic structure on the right.

60
00:03:19,696 --> 00:03:21,364
Until the 1930s,

61
00:03:21,364 --> 00:03:24,568
no one had ever seen a virus.

62
00:03:24,568 --> 00:03:29,773
So if our technology has advanced so much,

63
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:35,178
why are so many diseases still untreatable today?

64
00:03:35,178 --> 00:03:39,783
What I've learned is that technology isn't always the barrier.

65
00:03:39,783 --> 00:03:45,122
Sometimes it’s about the funding, the institutions and the incentives.

66
00:03:45,122 --> 00:03:47,691
We recently got a new malaria vaccine, as I mentioned,

67
00:03:47,691 --> 00:03:50,994
and when I first heard about it, I was amazed.

68
00:03:50,994 --> 00:03:52,562
I remember learning

69
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:55,832
that malaria was a very complicated disease, scientifically.

70
00:03:55,832 --> 00:03:59,336
It's caused by a parasite, not a virus or bacterium.

71
00:03:59,336 --> 00:04:03,740
And that parasite changes shape multiple times during its life cycle,

72
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:08,078
which makes it really hard to know what to target with a vaccine.

73
00:04:08,078 --> 00:04:12,082
So I wanted to write about it, this amazing breakthrough.

74
00:04:12,082 --> 00:04:15,418
But one of the first things I learned about this new malaria vaccine

75
00:04:15,418 --> 00:04:18,421
that was introduced just a few years ago,

76
00:04:18,421 --> 00:04:21,424
was that it was developed in the '90s.

77
00:04:21,424 --> 00:04:23,460
Decades ago.

78
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:27,197
The researchers who developed it, struggled to find funding to test it

79
00:04:27,197 --> 00:04:29,599
at every stage of the process.

80
00:04:29,599 --> 00:04:32,135
There was no commercial incentive.

81
00:04:32,135 --> 00:04:34,571
It's not profitable to develop new drugs

82
00:04:34,571 --> 00:04:36,740
and vaccines against diseases

83
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:39,709
that affect people in poverty, in poorer countries,

84
00:04:39,709 --> 00:04:42,746
even if millions of children might benefit.

85
00:04:42,746 --> 00:04:46,683
Even if there’s a huge economic benefit as well.

86
00:04:46,683 --> 00:04:50,587
It took foreign aid and philanthropy to fund the research to test it.

87
00:04:50,587 --> 00:04:55,392
And it literally took decades to reach the children who needed it.

88
00:04:55,392 --> 00:04:57,527
When I learned about this,

89
00:04:57,527 --> 00:05:00,297
I felt like there was nothing to celebrate.

90
00:05:00,297 --> 00:05:03,099
It seemed more like a failure.

91
00:05:03,099 --> 00:05:04,534
How could we let that happen?

92
00:05:04,534 --> 00:05:07,370
How could it take so long to test a vaccine

93
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:09,339
that had already been developed,

94
00:05:09,339 --> 00:05:13,343
while half a million children were dying from malaria every year?

95
00:05:13,343 --> 00:05:17,380
Why wasn't that the story?

96
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:21,251
Those children aren't coming back.

97
00:05:21,251 --> 00:05:27,157
But what I wanted to know was how we could prevent that from happening again.

98
00:05:27,157 --> 00:05:31,828
Like, what if we could change the economic incentives?

99
00:05:31,828 --> 00:05:36,032
Well, economists have come up with an idea to do just that.

100
00:05:36,032 --> 00:05:38,969
It’s called an “advanced market commitment.”

101
00:05:38,969 --> 00:05:43,340
It's where donors commit to buying a vaccine at a certain price per dose,

102
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:47,310
but only if it's developed and proven safe and effective.

103
00:05:47,310 --> 00:05:51,047
That commitment gives companies the confidence to invest in it

104
00:05:51,047 --> 00:05:52,716
in the first place,

105
00:05:52,716 --> 00:05:54,718
and it can bring vaccines into existence

106
00:05:54,718 --> 00:05:57,287
that would otherwise never get made.

107
00:05:57,287 --> 00:05:58,488
And most importantly,

108
00:05:58,488 --> 00:06:01,024
it makes sure that they're manufactured at scale

109
00:06:01,024 --> 00:06:03,059
and sold at an affordable price,

110
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:06,796
so they reach children who need them.

111
00:06:06,796 --> 00:06:08,765
That idea was used over a decade ago

112
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:11,534
to help develop new vaccines against pneumococcal disease,

113
00:06:11,534 --> 00:06:15,739
which is a deadly bacterial infection of the lungs.

114
00:06:15,739 --> 00:06:17,507
Vaccines already existed,

115
00:06:17,507 --> 00:06:23,146
but they didn't include the strains that were common in Africa and South Asia.

116
00:06:23,146 --> 00:06:27,083
So in 2009, several countries and philanthropists came together

117
00:06:27,083 --> 00:06:30,854
to fund an advanced market commitment for new vaccines.

118
00:06:30,854 --> 00:06:32,055
And it actually worked.

119
00:06:32,055 --> 00:06:33,890
Several companies developed them,

120
00:06:33,890 --> 00:06:36,726
and they reached children much faster than usual.

121
00:06:36,726 --> 00:06:40,530
It's estimated that those vaccines have saved over 700,000

122
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:42,465
children's lives since then.

123
00:06:42,465 --> 00:06:47,637
(Applause)

124
00:06:47,637 --> 00:06:53,643
This idea to fix market incentives is one way to speed up the process.

125
00:06:53,643 --> 00:06:55,946
But it's not the only thing we can do.

126
00:06:55,946 --> 00:06:58,548
Take childhood leukemia, for example.

127
00:06:58,548 --> 00:07:02,452
It used to be very difficult to test new treatments for the disease

128
00:07:02,452 --> 00:07:03,954
because leukemia is rare

129
00:07:03,954 --> 00:07:07,424
and individual hospitals struggled to find enough patients

130
00:07:07,424 --> 00:07:10,260
to run clinical trials.

131
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:12,529
So researchers built networks

132
00:07:12,529 --> 00:07:15,498
across the US and later Europe and Canada

133
00:07:15,498 --> 00:07:18,068
to help recruit patients from all over those countries

134
00:07:18,068 --> 00:07:20,603
into larger clinical trials.

135
00:07:20,603 --> 00:07:23,039
That collaboration made it possible

136
00:07:23,039 --> 00:07:27,077
to test treatments and learn what works faster.

137
00:07:27,077 --> 00:07:31,348
And it's why leukemia is no longer the disease it used to be.

138
00:07:31,348 --> 00:07:33,183
Before the 1970s,

139
00:07:33,183 --> 00:07:37,487
only around 15 percent of children with leukemia would survive

140
00:07:37,487 --> 00:07:40,190
even five years from a diagnosis.

141
00:07:40,190 --> 00:07:42,092
That's the bottom line on these graphs.

142
00:07:42,092 --> 00:07:43,960
Two types of leukemia.

143
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:46,463
Now the top line --

144
00:07:46,463 --> 00:07:49,499
that figure is 85 percent.

145
00:07:49,499 --> 00:07:52,402
Most children in richer countries today survive

146
00:07:52,402 --> 00:07:55,472
and are effectively cured of the disease.

147
00:07:55,472 --> 00:07:58,341
This graph is thousands of children

148
00:07:58,341 --> 00:08:00,910
who wouldn't have survived without that collaboration,

149
00:08:00,910 --> 00:08:02,545
without that progress.

150
00:08:02,545 --> 00:08:05,849
I want to give you another example.

151
00:08:05,849 --> 00:08:08,118
Back when I was at university,

152
00:08:08,118 --> 00:08:12,355
we had a substitute lecturer one day in my course on infectious diseases,

153
00:08:12,355 --> 00:08:15,592
and she told us that our original lecturer was away

154
00:08:15,592 --> 00:08:18,061
because he had gone to West Africa

155
00:08:18,061 --> 00:08:21,431
to do research to develop an Ebola vaccine.

156
00:08:21,431 --> 00:08:24,634
I remember sitting in that lecture theater at the back,

157
00:08:24,634 --> 00:08:27,437
and I was thinking to myself,

158
00:08:27,437 --> 00:08:29,439
can you just do that?

159
00:08:29,439 --> 00:08:32,042
Can you just pack up and go?

160
00:08:32,042 --> 00:08:34,878
It hadn't even occurred to me,

161
00:08:34,878 --> 00:08:38,415
and it made me realize that all of these breakthroughs

162
00:08:38,415 --> 00:08:39,949
came from people like him,

163
00:08:39,949 --> 00:08:43,219
people who changed their lives to make them happen,

164
00:08:43,219 --> 00:08:45,255
or people who thought differently,

165
00:08:45,255 --> 00:08:47,490
whether they were scientists, economists,

166
00:08:47,490 --> 00:08:49,726
operators or managers.

167
00:08:49,726 --> 00:08:52,762
They created the incentives and institutions

168
00:08:52,762 --> 00:08:55,865
so that generations in the future, all of us,

169
00:08:55,865 --> 00:08:59,436
wouldn't have to face the same problems that they did.

170
00:08:59,436 --> 00:09:02,272
And what's amazing is that we have an Ebola vaccine now

171
00:09:02,272 --> 00:09:04,574
against the most common strain.

172
00:09:04,574 --> 00:09:08,278
It wasn't made by my lecturer, but by researchers like him.

173
00:09:08,278 --> 00:09:11,314
And it was hard to develop and test it for all the same reasons.

174
00:09:11,314 --> 00:09:13,383
There was little commercial incentive,

175
00:09:13,383 --> 00:09:15,251
and it was hard to run clinical trials

176
00:09:15,251 --> 00:09:18,321
because it was hard to predict where Ebola outbreaks would arise

177
00:09:18,321 --> 00:09:20,857
and vaccinate people in advance.

178
00:09:20,857 --> 00:09:24,360
So scientists came up with a different idea.

179
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,697
They waited for individual cases of Ebola to appear

180
00:09:27,697 --> 00:09:30,467
and then quickly vaccinated all of the people around them

181
00:09:30,467 --> 00:09:32,402
[and] tested the vaccine that way.

182
00:09:32,402 --> 00:09:34,771
That idea, called ring vaccination,

183
00:09:34,771 --> 00:09:39,142
helped find a vaccine that we now know is very effective.

184
00:09:39,142 --> 00:09:44,781
Now these are just some of the ideas that people have had so far.

185
00:09:44,781 --> 00:09:48,418
But what I've learned from them is that breakthroughs don't just happen

186
00:09:48,418 --> 00:09:50,954
because we have the tools to make them.

187
00:09:50,954 --> 00:09:55,258
They depend on how innovation happens in the real world.

188
00:09:55,258 --> 00:09:57,794
And there's something else that I've learned from them

189
00:09:57,794 --> 00:10:00,497
that I find much harder to stomach.

190
00:10:00,497 --> 00:10:04,000
Many of them might not have happened at all.

191
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,238
So when I think back to that stream of innovation,

192
00:10:08,238 --> 00:10:11,574
all of those breakthroughs and all of that progress that we've seen,

193
00:10:11,574 --> 00:10:12,909
I do find it amazing.

194
00:10:12,909 --> 00:10:14,744
It's incredible.

195
00:10:14,744 --> 00:10:18,047
But it also really frustrates me.

196
00:10:18,047 --> 00:10:22,719
That's how much progress we've made despite it being so hard.

197
00:10:22,719 --> 00:10:26,456
The good news is people have come up with ideas to change that.

198
00:10:26,456 --> 00:10:29,592
Some of them really work, like the ones I've told you about today,

199
00:10:29,592 --> 00:10:32,695
and they just haven't been used enough.

200
00:10:32,695 --> 00:10:35,165
But we also need people to come up with new ideas

201
00:10:35,165 --> 00:10:38,001
and test them and scale them up too.

202
00:10:38,001 --> 00:10:40,470
I think it's more important than ever that we do it,

203
00:10:40,470 --> 00:10:43,907
because of cuts to science, global health and foreign aid,

204
00:10:43,907 --> 00:10:47,644
we have to find ways to make our resources go further.

205
00:10:47,644 --> 00:10:50,446
Progress isn't inevitable.

206
00:10:50,446 --> 00:10:54,317
It takes people who decide to persist despite that.

207
00:10:54,317 --> 00:10:56,019
And I think here it takes people

208
00:10:56,019 --> 00:10:59,255
who recognize something quite simple.

209
00:10:59,255 --> 00:11:02,525
That diseases are not a fact of life.

210
00:11:02,525 --> 00:11:04,928
They're problems that we can solve.

211
00:11:04,928 --> 00:11:06,196
Thank you.

212
00:11:06,196 --> 00:11:10,196
(Applause)
